#
# NARROWED 2026-07-27 (D53). This file used to carry two problems:
# the reservation asymmetry, and the contrast below. The first is
# settled -- bare words stay position-typed, the spec's line
# (syntax words reserved, domain vocabularies contextual) being the
# one Java and C# draw too. What survives is the second half, which
# that decision does not touch.
#
# PROBLEM: two grammatically identical lines, one naming something
# to CREATE and one naming something that must EXIST, with opposite
# failure behavior and nothing in the spelling to tell them apart.

description "Create versus must-exist"
platform    dos
machine     stopped
entry       install
timeout     30s

phase install {
    start
    wait   "C:\>"
    screenshot installed     # names an artifact to CREATE
    goto   finished          # names a phase that must EXIST
}

phase finished {
    finish
}

# Mistype the phase name and the parse fails -- `goto finsihed`
# names no declared phase. Mistype the artifact name and
# `screenshot instaled` succeeds, silently writing a file nobody
# will look for. Same shape, opposite consequence.
#
# The counter-argument, which is why this is a question and not a
# defect: naming an output and naming a reference ARE different
# acts, and getting the name you typed is how naming an output
# works everywhere. `screenshot` is closer to `> foo.txt` than to a
# variable reference. Under that reading there is nothing to fix in
# the semantics, and the complaint is only that the two look alike.
#
# OPEN QUESTION: is the look-alike worth spending anything on? The
# candidates, none costed:
#
#   - accept it -- creation and reference are distinguished by the
#     verb, which the author is already reading
#   - mark the creating position, so an artifact name is visibly
#     not a reference (a sigil costs G6's one spelling, and the
#     language is deliberately sigil-light)
#   - make the artifact name optional and derive it, so the common
#     case has no name to mistype
#
# Nothing here is urgent: no script has been written and debugged
# under this surface yet, which is the evidence the question is
# waiting on.
